@startuml

salt
{
  <b>ORE STUDIO - REPORTING: RUN A REPORT</b>
  --
  <b>DEFINITION: Group Consolidated Exposure - ACMCOR</b>
  --
  {#
    Type | Schedule | Scope | Pre-processing | Post-processing
    risk | scheduled 06:00 | portfolio (from the configuration) | execute | execute
  }
  --
  {
    [ Run now ] | Latest run: completed clean at 06:04:31
  }
  --
  <b>CONFIRM RUN</b>
  --
  {
    Run Group Consolidated Exposure now? Scope: portfolio (from the configuration).
    Pre-processing: execute. Post-processing: execute.
  }
  {
    [ Cancel ] | [ Run now ]
  }
  --
  <b>RUN 4b7e1a02 - RUNNING</b>
  --
  {#
    State | Started | Completed | Kind | Trigger
    running | 08:12:44 | - | from report_instance_kind | on demand
  }
  --
  <b>THE PIPELINE THIS RUN WILL WALK</b>
  --
  {#
    Phase | Step | Status
    Pre-processing | gather_trades | not reported
    Pre-processing | gather_market_data | not reported
    Pre-processing | assemble_bundle | not reported
    Pre-processing | prepare_ore_package | not reported
    Submission | submit_compute | not reported
    Execution | on a worker node - no service step | not reported
    Retrieval and Post-processing | collect_compute_results | not reported
    Finalisation | finalise_report | not reported
  }
  --
  The run's state is running. No reporting read says which step is current: the
  steps live in ores.workflow, and the screen draws the plan rather than a
  progress bar it cannot fill. A run whose definition substitutes pre-processing
  starts at resolve_prepared_input; one that ignores post-processing uses
  ignore_compute_results.
  --
  <b>TERMINAL: COMPLETED CLEAN</b>
  --
  {#
    State | Started | Completed | Took | Output message | Archive
    completed | 08:12:44 | 08:15:02 | 2 m 18 s | Report execution completed. | reporting/runs/<instance-id>/output.tar.gz
  }
  Health: (completed clean). Errors: none.
  --
  <b>TERMINAL: COMPLETED WHILE CARRYING ERRORS</b>
  --
  {#
    State | Completed | Output message | Errors | Archive
    completed | 08:15:02 | Report execution completed. | (11 errors) | reporting/runs/<instance-id>/output.tar.gz
  }
  The run succeeded and still carries errors. The panel says so and links to the
  error screen; it does not call the run failed.
  --
  <b>TERMINAL: FAILED</b>
  --
  {#
    State | Completed | Output message | Archive | Errors
    failed | 08:13:10 | - | none | link to the run's errors
  }
  A failed run has no archive to read. Its errors are read as one group and
  include the Report scope, the errors the model states itself when no engine saw
  the failure.
}

@enduml
